WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:02.997
This briefing comes from Data Platform,

3
00:00:02.997 --> 00:00:06.112
one of the books of the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

4
00:00:06.112 --> 00:00:08.725
It's about moving the
SQL Server estate from

5
00:00:08.725 --> 00:00:11.137
Windows Authentication to cloud identity.

6
00:00:11.137 --> 00:00:12.825
Here's why it gets its own book.

7
00:00:12.825 --> 00:00:16.278
Every identity program
eventually reaches the database,

8
00:00:16.278 --> 00:00:20.450
and that's where it finds the most
Windows authentication still in use.

9
00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:25.203
We'll go in four steps: why the database
is where legacy authentication hides,

10
00:00:25.203 --> 00:00:28.222
what replaces it, who owns
the work, and how to do it,

11
00:00:28.222 --> 00:00:32.975
from the first inventory of the estate
to a closed state you can keep proving.

12
00:00:32.975 --> 00:00:36.300
Let's start with how SQL
Server decides who you are.

13
00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:44.441
The short answer is that SQL Server
trusts whatever Windows tells it.

14
00:00:44.441 --> 00:00:48.994
A connection reaches the engine
in one of four ways: Kerberos,

15
00:00:48.994 --> 00:00:53.514
NTLM, a SQL login with a
password, or Entra authentication.

16
00:00:53.514 --> 00:00:56.616
Only Entra is ever seen
by Conditional Access.

17
00:00:56.616 --> 00:00:58.611
Three facts make this hard to see.

18
00:00:58.611 --> 00:01:02.201
You can't turn Windows
Authentication off in SQL Server,

19
00:01:02.201 --> 00:01:04.574
because the engine hands it to Windows.

20
00:01:04.574 --> 00:01:08.147
The protocol a connection used
isn't written to any log;

21
00:01:08.147 --> 00:01:10.490
you only see it in a live system view.

22
00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:16.453
And NTLM shows up silently, for example
when a service principal name is missing.

23
00:01:16.453 --> 00:01:18.318
The book is fair about one thing.

24
00:01:18.318 --> 00:01:22.512
Windows Authentication was good
engineering for a domain-joined estate.

25
00:01:22.512 --> 00:01:27.217
The case for replacing it is that that
world is ending, not that it was a mistake.

26
00:01:27.217 --> 00:01:29.050
So what's pushing it to end?

27
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.400
Three drivers make the change unavoidable.

28
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.413
First, NTLM is on its way out.

29
00:01:38.413 --> 00:01:44.374
Microsoft deprecated it in 2024 and
plans to disable it by default someday,

30
00:01:44.374 --> 00:01:50.337
and the program sets its own backstop,
April 1, 2027, for the domain-wide block.

31
00:01:50.337 --> 00:01:52.224
Second, Zero Trust.

32
00:01:52.224 --> 00:01:57.043
Federal policy puts phishing-resistant
MFA and the Entra identity plane

33
00:01:57.043 --> 00:02:01.625
in front of human access, and Windows
Authentication goes around both.

34
00:02:01.625 --> 00:02:05.347
Third, the directory modernization
in Identity and Directory

35
00:02:05.347 --> 00:02:09.213
ends the domain-joined world that
the service accounts depend on.

36
00:02:09.213 --> 00:02:12.337
There's no external NTLM deadline.

37
00:02:12.337 --> 00:02:18.090
The real dates are support dates for older
SQL Server and Windows Server versions,

38
00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:21.737
running through 2026 and 2027,
and the slide lists them.

39
00:02:21.737 --> 00:02:26.761
SQL Server 2014 and 2017 can't
use Entra authentication at all,

40
00:02:26.761 --> 00:02:31.412
so they only get to the closed
state by upgrade or retirement.

41
00:02:31.412 --> 00:02:34.030
The work starts with the estate itself.

42
00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:41.540
Before touching authentication, every
instance gets one of three verdicts.

43
00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:45.724
Retain it, if it's supported or can
be upgraded and carries real work.

44
00:02:45.724 --> 00:02:48.197
Consolidate it, moving its workload to a

45
00:02:48.197 --> 00:02:51.117
target platform and
retiring the old instance.

46
00:02:51.117 --> 00:02:54.184
Or retire it, if there's
no live workload left.

47
00:02:54.184 --> 00:02:58.073
The inventory has to cover the
whole forest, not one domain.

48
00:02:58.073 --> 00:03:01.601
Usage, versions and
dependencies are read together.

49
00:03:01.601 --> 00:03:06.188
And the book warns that usage counters
reset when SQL Server restarts,

50
00:03:06.188 --> 00:03:09.406
so they can't prove an instance
is unused on their own.

51
00:03:09.406 --> 00:03:14.334
The assessment uses the migration
tooling in SQL Server Management Studio,

52
00:03:14.334 --> 00:03:18.128
because the older Data Migration
Assistant has been retired.

53
00:03:18.128 --> 00:03:20.114
This is the gate for the whole program.

54
00:03:20.114 --> 00:03:22.571
Migrating an instance
that should have been

55
00:03:22.571 --> 00:03:25.296
retired is the most expensive
mistake it can make.

56
00:03:25.296 --> 00:03:29.250
Once we know what stays, each
instance needs a cloud identity.

57
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.844
What replaces the domain
join is Azure Arc,

58
00:03:35.844 --> 00:03:38.850
which gives each instance
an Entra identity.

59
00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:42.732
On SQL Server 2025, that's
a managed identity;

60
00:03:42.732 --> 00:03:46.912
on 2022, it's an app
registration with a certificate.

61
00:03:46.912 --> 00:03:50.213
The limits matter, and the
old documents missed several.

62
00:03:50.213 --> 00:03:54.512
Failover cluster instances can't
use Entra authentication at all.

63
00:03:54.512 --> 00:03:59.741
Entra authentication through Arc isn't
available in the US Government Virginia

64
00:03:59.741 --> 00:04:04.424
region, so a GCC tenant uses a commercial
subscription in the same tenant.

65
00:04:04.424 --> 00:04:09.087
The old runbook's government-cloud setup
would never have produced Entra logins.

66
00:04:09.087 --> 00:04:11.150
FIDO2 isn't supported here.

67
00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:15.132
And granting the instance its Graph
permissions takes a privileged

68
00:04:15.132 --> 00:04:18.275
role administrator, so that
step can't sit with the DBAs.

69
00:04:18.275 --> 00:04:22.810
Arc isn't the only path, but it wins
because it also brings inventory,

70
00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:28.087
policy, updates and Defender for the
host, which Devices and Endpoints covers.

71
00:04:28.087 --> 00:04:30.680
Next, the logins themselves.

72
00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:38.818
The logins move to Entra principals, and
they move in parallel, never in one jump.

73
00:04:38.818 --> 00:04:42.668
People become Entra users,
ideally granted through groups,

74
00:04:42.668 --> 00:04:46.755
so they pick up phishing-resistant
MFA and Conditional Access.

75
00:04:46.755 --> 00:04:49.355
Windows groups become Entra groups,

76
00:04:49.355 --> 00:04:54.643
with membership driven by HR attributes,
as HR-Driven Identity describes.

77
00:04:54.643 --> 00:04:58.330
Applications become service
principals and managed identities,

78
00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:02.755
and the book is clear that Conditional
Access doesn't cover managed identities.

79
00:05:02.755 --> 00:05:04.543
The parallel run is simple.

80
00:05:04.543 --> 00:05:09.067
Create the Entra user beside the
Windows user and copy its permissions.

81
00:05:09.067 --> 00:05:12.903
Keep both until the audit shows
no remaining Windows sessions,

82
00:05:12.903 --> 00:05:14.379
then disable the old one.

83
00:05:14.379 --> 00:05:17.488
The old runbook had a
one-step conversion shortcut,

84
00:05:17.488 --> 00:05:21.855
but it only works on Azure SQL
Managed Instance, not on SQL Server.

85
00:05:21.855 --> 00:05:26.008
And one trap: when you change the
instance's Entra administrator,

86
00:05:26.008 --> 00:05:29.142
the old one stays sysadmin
until a DBA removes it.

87
00:05:29.142 --> 00:05:30.920
So who does all this?

88
00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:36.304
The book's ownership chapter
separates deciding from doing.

89
00:05:36.304 --> 00:05:40.115
On the deciding side, the CIO
and data leadership decide

90
00:05:40.115 --> 00:05:44.266
whether each instance is
retained, consolidated or retired.

91
00:05:44.266 --> 00:05:48.119
The CISO, ISSO and
authorizing official decide

92
00:05:48.119 --> 00:05:52.834
exceptions for NTLM and SQL
logins, and sign off closure.

93
00:05:52.834 --> 00:05:57.069
Application and data owners pick
each application's cutover window.

94
00:05:57.069 --> 00:06:00.800
On the doing side, the data
platform team runs logins,

95
00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:03.404
roles, the audit and the parallel run.

96
00:06:03.404 --> 00:06:06.747
The identity team creates
Entra logins and groups,

97
00:06:06.747 --> 00:06:09.454
and grants the permissions the DBAs can't.

98
00:06:09.454 --> 00:06:11.796
The server team onboards hosts to Arc.

99
00:06:11.796 --> 00:06:15.494
Developers, the help desk and
security operations support.

100
00:06:15.494 --> 00:06:17.646
And watch for hidden administrators.

101
00:06:17.646 --> 00:06:22.684
Someone with contributor rights on an Arc
server is, in effect, an administrator

102
00:06:22.684 --> 00:06:27.273
of SQL Server on that host, and can
even switch password logins back on.

103
00:06:27.273 --> 00:06:29.924
Azure roles need the
same care as sysadmin.

104
00:06:29.924 --> 00:06:32.520
Now, the NTLM plan.

105
00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:39.445
NTLM is eliminated in three phases.

106
00:06:39.445 --> 00:06:44.699
First discover, with the authentication
audit and Windows NTLM logging.

107
00:06:44.699 --> 00:06:47.689
Then restrict: fix
service principal names,

108
00:06:47.689 --> 00:06:51.921
move connections to Kerberos or
Entra, and migrate what's left.

109
00:06:51.921 --> 00:06:57.680
Then eliminate, with the domain-wide block
on the program's April 2027 backstop.

110
00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:03.755
The highest LAN Manager authentication
level still accepts NTLM version two,

111
00:07:03.755 --> 00:07:08.992
so it isn't an NTLM block; the real
block is the Restrict NTLM policies.

112
00:07:08.992 --> 00:07:13.376
Their exceptions are server-name lists
on clients and domain controllers,

113
00:07:13.376 --> 00:07:15.762
not the program's own exception register.

114
00:07:15.762 --> 00:07:18.955
And Microsoft's upcoming NTLM change for

115
00:07:18.955 --> 00:07:22.757
Wi-Fi and VPN doesn't
touch SQL Server traffic.

116
00:07:22.757 --> 00:07:26.753
The good news is that the fastest
exit is the migration itself.

117
00:07:26.753 --> 00:07:31.496
An instance whose logins have moved
to Entra isn't affected by the block,

118
00:07:31.496 --> 00:07:35.355
so login migration and NTLM
elimination are the same work.

119
00:07:35.355 --> 00:07:38.030
Then access and applications follow.

120
00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:45.475
With the logins moved, access
is granted by attribute,

121
00:07:45.475 --> 00:07:47.743
and applications move by driver.

122
00:07:47.743 --> 00:07:51.870
On access, dynamic Entra
groups driven by HR attributes

123
00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:55.818
grant database access, so no
DBA hands it out manually.

124
00:07:55.818 --> 00:07:59.919
Privileged database roles go
through just-in-time elevation from

125
00:07:59.919 --> 00:08:04.243
Access and Privilege, and reviews
cover the groups, not just the logins.

126
00:08:04.243 --> 00:08:07.898
On applications, the book
sorts them into four classes,

127
00:08:07.898 --> 00:08:10.092
each with its own migration path.

128
00:08:10.092 --> 00:08:13.613
Connection strings name a
specific Entra authentication

129
00:08:13.613 --> 00:08:16.630
mode in production, rather
than a catch-all default.

130
00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:19.542
All the common database
drivers support Entra,

131
00:08:19.542 --> 00:08:22.230
and the book has a table
of minimum versions.

132
00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:24.455
The last mile is the connection string.

133
00:08:24.455 --> 00:08:29.767
Any application that still says Integrated
Security keeps the Windows path alive.

134
00:08:29.767 --> 00:08:34.005
So the application inventory is
part of closure, not a follow-up.

135
00:08:34.005 --> 00:08:36.610
And closure is the last big idea.

136
00:08:40.040 --> 00:08:43.668
The book's last idea is that a
transformation doesn't finish;

137
00:08:43.668 --> 00:08:47.591
it reaches a posture, and a posture
is something you keep proving.

138
00:08:47.591 --> 00:08:50.600
The closed state has five
conditions that hold together.

139
00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:56.148
Every in-scope instance uses Entra-only
authentication, SQL logins exist

140
00:08:56.148 --> 00:09:01.714
only as approved exceptions, and the
continuous audit shows no NTLM sessions.

141
00:09:01.714 --> 00:09:05.023
Any drift, like someone
switching back to mixed mode,

142
00:09:05.023 --> 00:09:07.330
is caught and treated as a violation.

143
00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:11.125
And the evidence is regenerated
from the instance's own audit,

144
00:09:11.125 --> 00:09:12.836
not assembled into a binder.

145
00:09:12.836 --> 00:09:15.057
One correction matters for assessors.

146
00:09:15.057 --> 00:09:19.758
Entra sign-in logs can't prove per-instance
access or the absence of NTLM,

147
00:09:19.758 --> 00:09:22.950
so the proof has to come
from the instance itself.

148
00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:25.160
And there's a certificate
layer underneath.

149
00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:31.016
SQL Server's TLS certificates usually
still come from AD Certificate Services,

150
00:09:31.016 --> 00:09:33.486
and Cloud PKI can't issue to servers.

151
00:09:33.486 --> 00:09:36.515
So the server certificate
issuer is its own decision.

152
00:09:36.515 --> 00:09:38.420
That leads to the asks.

153
00:09:42.170 --> 00:09:43.395
So, four asks.

154
00:09:43.395 --> 00:09:48.038
First, run the inventory and the
gate: a retain, consolidate or retire

155
00:09:48.038 --> 00:09:52.682
verdict for every instance before
any authentication work starts.

156
00:09:52.682 --> 00:09:57.565
Second, fund the Arc bridge, which
means a commercial subscription

157
00:09:57.565 --> 00:10:02.182
in the GCC tenant, with SQL Server
2022 or later as the target.

158
00:10:02.182 --> 00:10:07.648
Third, plan for the support dates,
upgrading or retiring the 2014,

159
00:10:07.648 --> 00:10:14.145
2016 and 2017 instances on time, because
they can't use Entra authentication.

160
00:10:14.145 --> 00:10:17.765
And fourth, decide the
certificate issuer for server TLS,

161
00:10:17.765 --> 00:10:22.233
because the AD Certificate Services
dependency needs its own answer.

162
00:10:22.233 --> 00:10:26.157
The database is where legacy
authentication hides longest.

163
00:10:26.157 --> 00:10:29.835
Bringing it into Entra is what
finally lets Conditional Access,

164
00:10:29.835 --> 00:10:33.720
phishing-resistant sign-in and
continuous evidence reach the data.

165
00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:36.909
Data Platform has the
narrative and ten runbooks,

166
00:10:36.909 --> 00:10:39.320
with every external claim footnoted.

167
00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:42.365
Devices and Endpoints
covers Arc on servers,

168
00:10:42.365 --> 00:10:46.232
and Access and Privilege the
access policy it plugs into.

169
00:10:46.232 --> 00:10:47.060
Thank you.
